window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-ecosystem","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Ecosystem · Practice","chapter":"Ecosystem","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Of the photosynthetically active radiation (PAR) falling on them, plants are able to capture only about","image":"","options":["50–60 per cent","20–30 per cent","2–10 per cent","0.01–0.1 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that plants capture only 2–10 per cent of the PAR.\nThis small amount of energy sustains the entire living world.\nThe figure 'less than 50 per cent' describes how much of the incident solar radiation is PAR. It is not the share of PAR that plants capture, so the 50–60 per cent option is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Species composition and stratification are the two main structural features of an ecosystem.\nStatement II: Like energy flow, nutrient flow in an ecosystem is unidirectional, and nutrients once used are lost from the system as heat.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT names species composition and stratification as the two main structural features of an ecosystem.\nStatement II is incorrect. Only energy flows in one direction and is lost as heat.\nNutrients are stored and moved through the components of the ecosystem and are used repeatedly through nutrient cycling.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT's pyramid of numbers for a grassland ecosystem, about how many top carnivores are supported by the production of nearly 6 million plants?","image":"","options":["3","30","300","3000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Figure 12.4(a) shows a grassland pyramid of numbers.\nNearly 6 million producers (plants) support progressively fewer herbivores and primary carnivores.\nAt the apex, only 3 top carnivores are supported.\nThe numbers fall so sharply because energy decreases at each successive trophic level.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (steps of decomposition) with List-II (description):\nList-I:\n(A) Fragmentation\n(B) Leaching\n(C) Catabolism\n(D) Mineralisation\nList-II:\n(i) Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances\n(ii) Release of inorganic nutrients when humus is degraded by microbes\n(iii) Detritivores break detritus into smaller particles\n(iv) Water-soluble inorganic nutrients go down the soil horizon and precipitate as unavailable salts\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Fragmentation matches (iii): detritivores such as earthworms break detritus into smaller particles.\n(B) Leaching matches (iv): soluble nutrients move down the soil and get precipitated as unavailable salts.\n(C) Catabolism matches (i): bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus.\n(D) Mineralisation matches (ii): microbes degrade humus and release inorganic nutrients.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the rate of function of the entire pond ecosystem is regulated by:","image":"","options":["Solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions","Only the number of decomposers present at the bottom","Only the amount of soil deposited at the bottom","The number of bottom-dwelling consumers alone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the solar input, the cycle of temperature, day-length and other climatic conditions regulate the rate of function of the whole pond.\nDecomposers, soil and consumers are components of the pond, but none of them alone sets the overall rate.\nThe tempting options all name a single component in place of the governing climatic and energy inputs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the grazing food chain.\nReason (R): In an aquatic ecosystem, the grazing food chain is the major conduit for energy flow.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. On land, most plant biomass is not grazed. It becomes detritus, so most energy flows through the detritus food chain.\nR is also true. NCERT states that the grazing food chain is the major conduit in aquatic ecosystems.\nHowever, R describes a different ecosystem. It does not explain why detritus dominates on land.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The number of trophic levels in a grazing food chain is limited.\nReason (R): Only about 10 per cent of the energy at one trophic level is transferred to the next higher trophic level.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a grazing food chain usually has only producer, herbivore, primary carnivore and secondary carnivore levels.\nR is true: this is the 10 per cent law of energy transfer.\nSince about 90% of the energy is lost at each step, too little remains after a few steps to support another level.\nThus R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about decomposers are correct?\n(i) They are heterotrophic organisms.\n(ii) They secrete digestive enzymes that break down dead and waste materials.\n(iii) They absorb the simple inorganic materials formed after breakdown.\n(iv) They meet their energy needs by trapping solar energy.\n(v) They are also known as saprotrophs.","image":"","options":["Five","Three","Four","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: decomposers are heterotrophs, mainly fungi and bacteria.\n(ii) Correct: they secrete digestive enzymes on dead and waste matter.\n(iii) Correct: the simple inorganic materials released are then absorbed by them.\n(iv) Incorrect: they get energy by degrading detritus, not by trapping sunlight.\n(v) Correct: 'sapro' means to decompose, so they are called saprotrophs.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT's description of forest stratification, which vertical layer is occupied by shrubs?","image":"","options":["Top layer","Bottom layer","Second layer","They have no fixed layer and occur at all levels"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes three strata in a forest.\nTrees occupy the top vertical stratum.\nShrubs occupy the second layer.\nHerbs and grasses occupy the bottom layers.\nThe top layer belongs to trees, so shrubs fit only the second layer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"During decomposition in the soil, a dark coloured, amorphous substance that is highly resistant to microbial action accumulates. Which process leads to the accumulation of this substance?","image":"","options":["Fragmentation","Leaching","Humification","Mineralisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The dark coloured, amorphous substance that resists microbial action is humus.\nNCERT states that humification leads to the accumulation of humus.\nMineralisation is a different step. It degrades humus further and releases inorganic nutrients, so it does not build humus up.\nFragmentation is done by detritivores, and leaching moves soluble nutrients downward. Neither forms humus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about humus is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a light coloured crystalline substance that is decomposed rapidly by microbes","Being colloidal in nature, it acts as a reservoir of nutrients","It is formed by leaching of water-soluble nutrients into deeper soil","It decomposes faster than fresh leaf litter rich in sugars"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: humus is dark coloured and amorphous, and it is highly resistant to microbial action.\n(2) Correct: humus is colloidal in nature, so it serves as a reservoir of nutrients.\n(3) Incorrect: humus forms by humification, not by leaching.\n(4) Incorrect: humus decomposes at an extremely slow rate, whereas sugar-rich litter decomposes quickly.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"A grassland has a gross primary productivity of 20,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$. The producers use 8,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$ in respiration. What is the net primary productivity available to heterotrophs?","image":"","options":["28,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","12,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","8,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","2,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: GPP = 20,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$ and R = 8,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$.\nFormula: NPP = GPP − R.\nSubstituting: NPP = 20,000 − 8,000.\nSo NPP = 12,000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$. This is the biomass available to herbivores and decomposers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"The annual NPP of the biosphere is about 170 billion tons (dry weight), of which oceans contribute about 55 billion tons. The approximate annual NPP contributed by land is:","image":"","options":["55 billion tons","225 billion tons","115 billion tons","70 billion tons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: total NPP = 170 billion tons and ocean NPP = 55 billion tons.\nNCERT says the rest of the NPP comes from land.\nLand NPP = 170 − 55 = 115 billion tons.\nOption (2) wrongly adds the two figures instead of subtracting.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following act as producers in an aquatic ecosystem such as a pond?\nPhytoplankton, algae, submerged higher plants, zooplankton, molluscs, fungi","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Producers (autotrophs) in aquatic ecosystems are phytoplankton, algae and higher plants. These include floating, submerged and marginal plants.\nZooplankton are consumers.\nMolluscs are herbivores, i.e., primary consumers.\nFungi are decomposers.\nSo 3 of the six listed organisms are producers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The dissolved organic substances present in pond water form part of the biotic component of the pond.\nStatement II: Both submerged plants and marginal plants at the edges of a pond are autotrophic components.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT includes the water with its dissolved inorganic and organic substances in the abiotic component. Being organic does not make a non-living substance biotic.\nStatement II is correct. Floating, submerged and marginal plants are all listed as autotrophs of the pond.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which two climatic factors are the most important in regulating the rate of decomposition, through their effects on the activities of soil microbes?","image":"","options":["Light intensity and wind speed","Temperature and soil moisture","Day-length and humidity of air","Soil pH and altitude"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names temperature and soil moisture as the most important climatic factors that regulate decomposition.\nThey act by influencing the activity of soil microbes.\nLight and day-length affect photosynthesis, not microbial breakdown of detritus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about energy flow in ecosystems are correct?\n(i) The sun is the energy source for every ecosystem on Earth, without any exception.\n(ii) Energy flows unidirectionally from the sun to producers and then to consumers.\n(iii) Ecosystems require a constant energy input to counteract the tendency toward increasing disorderliness.\n(iv) More than half of the incident solar radiation is photosynthetically active radiation.\n(v) Besides green plants, photosynthetic bacteria can also fix the sun's radiant energy to make food from simple inorganic materials.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem does not depend on the sun.\n(ii) Correct: energy flow is unidirectional, from sun to producers to consumers.\n(iii) Correct: this follows from the second law of thermodynamics.\n(iv) Incorrect: PAR is less than 50 per cent of the incident radiation.\n(v) Correct: NCERT lists both plants and photosynthetic bacteria as autotrophs that fix radiant energy.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Less than half of the incident solar radiation is photosynthetically active radiation.\nStatement II: Plants and photosynthetic bacteria fix the Sun's radiant energy to make food from simple inorganic materials.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that less than 50 per cent of incident solar radiation is PAR.\nStatement II is correct: NCERT names both plants and photosynthetic bacteria as autotrophs that fix radiant energy to make food.\nSo both statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"During decomposition, some microbes further degrade humus, and inorganic nutrients are released. This step is called","image":"","options":["fragmentation","leaching","humification","mineralisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Humification produces humus, which accumulates in the soil.\nSome microbes then degrade this humus and release inorganic nutrients. This step is mineralisation.\nFragmentation is the breakdown of detritus into smaller particles by detritivores.\nLeaching carries water-soluble nutrients down the soil horizon.\nHumification is tempting, but it forms humus rather than releasing nutrients from it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a terrestrial ecosystem, the major producers are","image":"","options":["Phytoplankton and algae","Herbaceous and woody plants","Fungi and bacteria","Insects and molluscs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, the major producers on land are herbaceous and woody plants.\nPhytoplankton and algae are producers in aquatic ecosystems, not the major producers on land.\nFungi and bacteria are decomposers.\nInsects and molluscs are herbivores, i.e., primary consumers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following conditions would slow down the rate of decomposition?\n(i) Low temperature\n(ii) Anaerobiosis\n(iii) Detritus rich in lignin\n(iv) Detritus rich in chitin\n(v) Warm and moist environment\n(vi) Detritus rich in nitrogen","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Slows: low temperature inhibits decomposition.\n(ii) Slows: anaerobiosis inhibits decomposition.\n(iii) Slows: lignin-rich detritus decomposes slowly.\n(iv) Slows: chitin-rich detritus decomposes slowly.\n(v) Speeds up: a warm, moist environment favours decomposition.\n(vi) Speeds up: nitrogen-rich detritus decomposes quickly.\nSo four conditions slow decomposition.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: In a forest, trees occupy the top vertical layer, shrubs the second, and herbs and grasses the bottom layers. This vertical distribution is called stratification.\nStatement II: In a pond ecosystem, decomposers such as fungi, bacteria and flagellates are especially abundant at the bottom of the pond.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT calls the vertical distribution of species at different levels stratification. Trees form the top layer, shrubs the second, and herbs and grasses the bottom.\nStatement II: Correct. In the NCERT pond example, fungi, bacteria and flagellates are the decomposers. They are especially abundant at the bottom, where dead matter settles.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"The mass of living material present at a trophic level at a particular time is called its","image":"","options":["Net primary productivity","Detritus","Standing crop","Secondary productivity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines standing crop as the mass of living material at a trophic level at a particular time.\nIt is measured as biomass or as number per unit area.\nProductivity is a rate of production over time, so NPP and secondary productivity do not fit.\nDetritus is dead organic matter, not living material.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of natural aquatic ecosystems?\nPond, grassland, estuary, wetland, desert, river, crop field","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aquatic examples in NCERT: pond, wetland, river and estuary. That makes 4.\nGrassland and desert are terrestrial ecosystems.\nA crop field is a man-made ecosystem, not a natural aquatic one.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The figure of about 170 billion tons of organic matter per year for the biosphere represents its gross primary productivity.\nStatement II: The oceans' share of about 55 billion tons represents the biomass available to marine herbivores and decomposers.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT gives 170 billion tons as the annual net primary productivity (NPP), not the GPP.\nNPP = GPP − R, so the GPP must be larger than this figure.\nStatement II is correct. NPP is the biomass available to heterotrophs, that is, herbivores and decomposers.\nSo the 55 billion tons of marine NPP is what supports marine consumers.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: Ecosystems vary greatly in size, ranging from a small pond to a large forest or a sea.\nStatement II: An ecosystem includes only the living organisms of an area and their interactions with one another.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that ecosystem size ranges from a small pond to a large forest or sea.\nStatement II is incorrect. An ecosystem also includes the physical environment.\nLiving organisms interact with abiotic components as well as with each other.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In an ideal ecosystem, 1,000,000 J of sunlight energy is available to the producers. The producers convert 1% of it into NPP, and the 10 per cent law applies to every later transfer. How much energy reaches the tertiary consumers?","image":"","options":["100 J","1 J","1000 J","10 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: sunlight = $10^6$ J; sunlight to NPP = 1%; transfer between trophic levels = 10%.\nStep 1: producers (NPP) = 1% of $10^6$ J = $10^4$ J.\nStep 2: primary consumers = $10^3$ J.\nStep 3: secondary consumers = $10^2$ J.\nStep 4: tertiary consumers (4th trophic level) = 10 J.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Primary production and productivity are expressed in different units. Which unit is used to compare the productivity of different ecosystems?","image":"","options":["g $m^{-2}$","kcal $m^{-2}$","g $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","kg of fresh weight per plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Primary production is the amount of biomass produced per unit area over a time period. It is expressed as weight (g $m^{-2}$) or energy (kcal $m^{-2}$). \nProductivity is the rate of biomass production, so it must include time: g $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$ or (kcal $m^{-2}$) $yr^{-1}$. \nOptions (1) and (2) have no time component, so they measure production, not productivity. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In any ecosystem, NPP is less than GPP.\n(ii) Expressing productivity in (kcal $m^{-2}$) $yr^{-1}$ allows the productivity of different ecosystems to be compared.\n(iii) Of the roughly 170 billion tons (dry weight) of annual NPP of the biosphere, oceans contribute only about 55 billion tons.\n(iv) Secondary productivity refers to organic matter formed by autotrophs.\n(v) Primary production is expressed in g $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: part of GPP is always lost in respiration, so NPP < GPP. \n(ii) Correct: a rate per unit area per year lets different ecosystems be compared. \n(iii) Correct: these are the figures given in NCERT. \n(iv) Incorrect: secondary productivity is due to consumers. \n(v) Incorrect: g $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$ is the unit of productivity; production is in g $m^{-2}$. \nSo three statements are correct. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT, the rate of biomass production in an ecosystem (productivity) is expressed in which unit, so that different ecosystems can be compared?","image":"","options":["g m$^{-2}$","g m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","kcal m$^{-2}$","g m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Primary production is an amount of biomass, so it is expressed as weight (g m$^{-2}$) or energy (kcal m$^{-2}$).\nProductivity is a rate, so it needs a time component: g m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$ or (kcal m$^{-2}$) yr$^{-1}$.\ng m$^{-2}$ and kcal m$^{-2}$ have no time term, so they measure production, not productivity.\ng m$^{-3}$ is a volume-based unit and is not used here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (step of decomposition) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\nA. Fragmentation\nB. Leaching\nC. Catabolism\nD. Mineralisation\nList-II:\n(i) Water-soluble inorganic nutrients move down the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts\n(ii) Detritivores break detritus down into smaller particles\n(iii) Inorganic nutrients are released when microbes degrade humus\n(iv) Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): In fragmentation, detritivores such as earthworms break detritus into small particles.\nB-(i): In leaching, soluble nutrients sink into the soil horizon and precipitate as unavailable salts.\nC-(iv): In catabolism, bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances.\nD-(iii): In mineralisation, microbes degrade humus and release inorganic nutrients.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In an aquatic ecosystem, the grazing food chain is the major conduit for energy flow.\nStatement II: The death of an organism marks the beginning of the grazing food chain.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the GFC is the major conduit for energy flow in aquatic ecosystems.\nStatement II is incorrect. Death of an organism is the beginning of the detritus food chain/web, which starts with dead organic matter.\nThe grazing food chain starts with living producers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT gives one group as common herbivores (primary consumers) in aquatic ecosystems. Which group is it?","image":"","options":["Molluscs","Saprotrophic fungi","Phytoplankton","Flagellates of the pond bottom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT lists insects, birds and mammals as common terrestrial herbivores, and molluscs as aquatic herbivores.\nPhytoplankton are producers, not consumers.\nFungi and flagellates at the pond bottom are decomposers.\nSo molluscs are the primary consumers here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In an ideal energy pyramid, producers receive 2,000,000 J of sunlight energy. They convert only 1% of it into net primary productivity (NPP). Energy then passes up the grazing food chain according to the 10 per cent law. How much energy reaches the secondary carnivores (fourth trophic level)?","image":"","options":["2000 J","200 J","20 J","2 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: sunlight available = 2,000,000 J, producer efficiency = 1%, and 10% transfer at each step.\nFormula: NPP = 1% of sunlight; each next level receives 10% of the level below.\nSubstituting: NPP = 20,000 J, so herbivores get 2000 J and primary carnivores get 200 J.\nSo secondary carnivores receive 10% of 200 J = 20 J.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"An ecologist surveys a grassland and prepares a complete list identifying and counting all the plant and animal species found there. Which structural feature of the ecosystem does this list describe?","image":"","options":["Species composition","Stratification","Standing crop","Productivity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that identifying and enumerating the plant and animal species of an ecosystem gives its species composition.\nSpecies composition and stratification are the two main structural features of an ecosystem.\nStratification deals with vertical layering, not a species list.\nStanding crop is the biomass present at a trophic level, and productivity is a rate of biomass production.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a grassland, the gross primary productivity is 20,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$ and the respiratory loss of the plants is 12,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$. What is the net primary productivity?","image":"","options":["32,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","12,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","8,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","1.67 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: GPP = 20,000 and R = 12,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\nFormula: NPP = GPP − R.\nSubstituting: NPP = 20,000 − 12,000.\nSo NPP = 8,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\nAdding the two values (32,000) or dividing them (1.67) is not how NPP is obtained.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Primary productivity of an ecosystem depends on the plant species present as well as on environmental factors, nutrient availability and the photosynthetic capacity of the plants.\nStatement II: Since all ecosystems receive solar energy, primary productivity is nearly uniform across different types of ecosystems.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT lists the plant species, environmental factors, nutrient availability and photosynthetic capacity as the factors that determine primary productivity.\nStatement II is incorrect: because these factors differ from place to place, productivity varies among ecosystems.\nFor example, the oceans cover about 70% of Earth's surface yet produce only 55 of the 170 billion tons of annual NPP.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a pond ecosystem, the decomposers (fungi, bacteria and flagellates) are especially abundant in which region?","image":"","options":["The surface layer of water exposed to sunlight","The bottom of the pond","Among the floating plants at the centre of the pond","In the open middle water column with zooplankton"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names fungi, bacteria and flagellates as the decomposers of a pond.\nIt states that they are especially abundant in the bottom of the pond.\nDead organic matter settles there, so the raw material for decomposition collects at the bottom.\nThe surface layer is tempting because it is the most productive zone, but it is dominated by phytoplankton (autotrophs), not decomposers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a grassland, the net primary productivity of the plants is 10,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$. A sparrow eats seeds of these plants and also eats insects that feed only on the same plants. Assume the 10 per cent law holds. How much energy is available, per m$^2$ per year, at the trophic level the sparrow occupies when it feeds on the insects?","image":"","options":["1000 kcal","100 kcal","10 kcal","1 kcal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: producer NPP = 10,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$. Only 10% of energy passes to each next trophic level.\nThe insects are herbivores at the 2nd trophic level, so they receive 10% of 10,000 = 1000 kcal.\nA sparrow eating insects is a secondary consumer at the 3rd trophic level, so it receives 10% of 1000 = 100 kcal.\nThe 1000 kcal value applies only when the sparrow eats seeds as a primary consumer. A species can occupy more than one trophic level at the same time.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"The decomposers that make up the detritus food chain are mainly","image":"","options":["earthworms and millipedes","phytoplankton and algae","zooplankton","fungi and bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the detritus food chain is made up of decomposers, which are heterotrophic organisms, mainly fungi and bacteria.\nEarthworms are detritivores. They fragment detritus but are not the main decomposers.\nPhytoplankton and algae are producers, and zooplankton are consumers.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which description best fits the term ecosystem?","image":"","options":["A group of individuals of one species living in a defined area","A functional unit of nature in which living organisms interact among themselves and with their physical environment","All the populations of different species living in an area, without their non-living surroundings","Only the non-living physical surroundings of a region, such as soil, water and air"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes an ecosystem as a functional unit of nature.\nIn it, living organisms interact with each other and with the surrounding physical (abiotic) environment.\nOption (1) describes a population, and option (3) describes a community, which leaves out the abiotic part.\nOption (4) covers only the abiotic part.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although oceans cover about 70% of the Earth's surface, they contribute less than one-third of the annual NPP of the biosphere.\nReason (R): Light penetrates only the upper layers of ocean water, which limits photosynthesis by producers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Oceans give 55 out of 170 billion tons, which is about 32%, slightly less than one-third.\nR is true. Light decreases sharply with depth, so photosynthesis is confined to the surface layers.\nLimited light lowers primary production over most of the ocean volume, so R correctly explains why the oceans' share is small.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following constitute detritus, the raw material for decomposition?\nFallen dry leaves\nShed bark\nWithered flowers\nDead remains of animals\nFecal matter\nLiving phytoplankton floating in a pond","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Detritus consists of dead plant remains (leaves, bark, flowers) and dead animal remains, including fecal matter.\nFallen leaves, shed bark, withered flowers, animal remains and fecal matter are all detritus. That makes 5 items.\nLiving phytoplankton are producers, not dead organic matter, so they are not detritus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are terrestrial ecosystems?\nForest, wetland, grassland, river, desert, lake, estuary","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Forest: terrestrial.\nGrassland: terrestrial.\nDesert: terrestrial.\nWetland, river, lake and estuary: all aquatic, as listed by NCERT.\nThe wetland is the most tempting mistake, but NCERT groups it with aquatic ecosystems.\nSo the count of terrestrial ecosystems is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Detritivores such as earthworms break detritus into smaller particles. This step of decomposition is called:","image":"","options":["Catabolism","Leaching","Fragmentation","Mineralisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When detritivores break detritus down into smaller pieces, the step is called fragmentation.\nCatabolism is a different step: it is the enzymatic breakdown carried out by bacteria and fungi.\nLeaching is the downward movement of water-soluble nutrients into the soil.\nMineralisation is the release of inorganic nutrients from humus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about ecosystems is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Interaction of biotic and abiotic components produces a physical structure characteristic of each type of ecosystem","A pond, though small, is a fairly self-sustaining unit that shows the basic functions of an ecosystem","Wetlands and estuaries are grouped with aquatic ecosystems, while grasslands and deserts are terrestrial","An aquarium cannot be considered an ecosystem because, being man-made, it lacks an abiotic component"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Biotic–abiotic interaction gives each ecosystem its characteristic physical structure.\n(2) Correct. NCERT uses the pond as a fairly self-sustainable example showing all the basic ecosystem functions.\n(3) Correct. This matches NCERT's lists of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems.\n(4) Incorrect. NCERT does treat an aquarium as a man-made ecosystem. Its water, dissolved substances and light are its abiotic components.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Four detritus samples are kept under different conditions. Which sample is expected to decompose at the fastest rate?","image":"","options":["Lignin-rich wood chips in warm, moist, well-aerated soil","Nitrogen-rich leaves in cold, waterlogged soil","Nitrogen- and sugar-rich leaf litter in warm, moist, well-aerated soil","Chitin-rich crustacean shells in warm, moist, well-aerated soil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Decomposition is fastest when both the detritus and the climate are favourable.\nOn chemistry: nitrogen and sugar-rich detritus decomposes quickly, while lignin and chitin decompose slowly.\nOn climate: warm, moist, aerated soil favours decomposition, while cold, anaerobic (waterlogged) soil inhibits it.\nOption 3 combines favourable chemistry with favourable climate.\nOptions 1 and 4 have resistant detritus, and option 2 has inhibiting conditions despite favourable chemistry.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Desert\n(B) Estuary\n(C) Aquarium\n(D) Biosphere\nList-II:\n(I) Man-made ecosystem\n(II) Global ecosystem\n(III) Natural terrestrial ecosystem\n(IV) Natural aquatic ecosystem\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Desert is a natural terrestrial ecosystem, so A-III.\n(B) Estuary is a natural aquatic ecosystem, so B-IV.\n(C) Aquarium is man-made, so C-I.\n(D) Biosphere is the composite of all local ecosystems, that is, the global ecosystem, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I:\n(A) Photosynthetically active radiation (PAR) as part of incident solar radiation\n(B) Fraction of PAR captured by plants\n(C) Sunlight energy available to primary producers that is converted into NPP (ideal energy pyramid)\n(D) Energy transferred from one trophic level to the next\nList-II:\n(i) About 10%\n(ii) Less than 50%\n(iii) 2–10%\n(iv) About 1%\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Less than 50% of incident solar radiation is PAR, so A matches (ii).\n(B) Plants capture only 2–10% of PAR, so B matches (iii).\n(C) In the ideal energy pyramid, producers convert only about 1% of available sunlight into NPP, so C matches (iv).\n(D) Under the 10 per cent law, about 10% passes to the next level, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a grassland food chain, a grasshopper feeds directly on grass. Which trophic level does the grasshopper occupy?","image":"","options":["First trophic level","Second trophic level","Third trophic level","Fourth trophic level"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Producers such as grass occupy the first trophic level.\nHerbivores, or primary consumers, occupy the second trophic level.\nThe grasshopper eats grass directly, so it is a herbivore at the second level.\nThe third level belongs to carnivores, which are secondary consumers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In an ecosystem, the biomass available for consumption by heterotrophs (herbivores and decomposers) is represented by:","image":"","options":["Gross primary productivity","Net primary productivity","Secondary productivity","Respiration loss of producers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"GPP is the total organic matter made during photosynthesis. A considerable part of it is used up by the plants themselves in respiration. \nWhat remains (GPP − R = NPP) is the biomass available to herbivores and decomposers. \nGPP is tempting, but its respired portion never reaches consumers. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the grazing food chain.\nStatement II: Although oceans cover about 70 per cent of Earth's surface, they contribute more than half of the annual net primary productivity of the biosphere.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that in terrestrial ecosystems most energy flows through the detritus food chain. In aquatic ecosystems, the grazing food chain is the major conduit.\nStatement II is incorrect. Of about 170 billion tons of annual NPP, oceans produce only about 55 billion tons. That is roughly one-third, which is less than half.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Measuring the biomass of a trophic level in terms of dry weight is considered more accurate than fresh weight because","image":"","options":["Dry weight includes the mass of water stored in tissues","Water content of organisms varies greatly, while dry weight reflects actual organic matter","Dry weight can be measured without removing organisms from the ecosystem","Fresh weight ignores the mass of the producers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fresh weight includes water, and water content changes with species, season and hydration.\nDry weight removes this variable and represents the true organic matter.\nThis organic matter is what carries energy to the next trophic level.\nOption 1 is wrong because dry weight excludes water.\nOption 3 is wrong because drying requires the organisms to be collected.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is an example of a natural aquatic ecosystem?","image":"","options":["Grassland","Desert","Estuary","Aquarium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists pond, lake, wetland, river and estuary as aquatic ecosystems.\nGrassland and desert are terrestrial ecosystems.\nAn aquarium holds water but is man-made, not natural.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of a pond ecosystem) with List-II (example):\nList-I\n(A) Autotroph\n(B) Consumer\n(C) Decomposer\n(D) Abiotic component\nList-II\n(I) Flagellates\n(II) Dissolved organic substances in water\n(III) Marginal plants\n(IV) Free-swimming forms","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-III: Marginal plants at the pond edges are autotrophs.\nB-IV: Free-swimming forms are consumers.\nC-I: Flagellates are decomposers, together with fungi and bacteria.\nD-II: Dissolved organic substances are part of the abiotic water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about ecological pyramids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In most ecosystems, the pyramids of number, biomass and energy are all upright.","An inverted pyramid of biomass in the sea means that the zooplankton level holds more energy than the phytoplankton level.","Calculations of energy, biomass or numbers at a trophic level must include all organisms at that level.","Each bar of an energy pyramid shows the energy present at a trophic level in a given time or annually per unit area."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in most ecosystems all three pyramids are upright.\n(2) Incorrect: the inverted biomass pyramid compares standing crop only. Energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher one, so the energy pyramid of the sea is still upright.\n(3) Correct: generalisations fail if only a few individuals are counted.\n(4) Correct: this is how NCERT defines each energy bar.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: A sparrow functions as a primary consumer when it eats seeds and fruits, and as a secondary consumer when it eats insects. This shows that a trophic level is a functional level rather than a species.\nStatement II: Ecological pyramids readily accommodate a species that belongs to two or more trophic levels, and they represent food webs accurately.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT uses the sparrow to show that one species can occupy more than one trophic level at the same time. A trophic level is a functional level.\nStatement II: Incorrect. This describes the opposite of the NCERT limitations of ecological pyramids. Pyramids do not account for a species at two or more levels. They assume a simple food chain and do not accommodate a food web.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Organic matter tends to build up in soils of very cold regions and in waterlogged areas.\nStatement II: Humification rapidly converts detritus into inorganic nutrients that are immediately available to plants.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit decomposition, which results in the build-up of organic materials.\nStatement II is incorrect. Humification produces humus, which is highly resistant to microbes and decomposes extremely slowly.\nNutrients are released from humus only later, through mineralisation by some microbes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a part of the abiotic component of a pond?","image":"","options":["Dissolved inorganic substances in the water","Rich soil deposit at the bottom","Dissolved organic substances in the water","Zooplankton suspended in the water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the abiotic component of a pond in three parts:\n(i) the water itself,\n(ii) the dissolved inorganic and organic substances in it, and\n(iii) the rich soil deposit at the bottom.\nZooplankton are living organisms that feed on other organisms, so they are biotic consumers, not abiotic.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the grazing food chain: Grass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake → Hawk.\nWhich of the following descriptions is correct?","image":"","options":["The grasshopper is a secondary consumer at the second trophic level.","The snake is a primary carnivore at the third trophic level.","The hawk is a tertiary consumer at the fourth trophic level.","The frog is a primary carnivore and a secondary consumer at the third trophic level."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Grass is the producer (1st trophic level). The grasshopper is the primary consumer, a herbivore (2nd).\nThe frog eats the herbivore, so it is a primary carnivore, the secondary consumer, at the 3rd level. This makes option 4 correct.\nThe snake is a secondary carnivore and tertiary consumer at the 4th level.\nThe hawk is at the 5th level.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (step of decomposition) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(A) Fragmentation\n(B) Leaching\n(C) Catabolism\n(D) Humification\nList-II:\n(I) Degradation of detritus into simpler inorganic substances by bacterial and fungal enzymes\n(II) Accumulation of a dark, amorphous substance resistant to microbial action\n(III) Breakdown of detritus into smaller particles by detritivores\n(IV) Downward movement of water-soluble nutrients that get precipitated as unavailable salts","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Match each step to its description:\nFragmentation is the breaking of detritus into small particles by detritivores, so A–III.\nLeaching is the downward movement of soluble nutrients, which precipitate as unavailable salts, so B–IV.\nCatabolism is degradation by bacterial and fungal enzymes, so C–I.\nHumification is the formation of dark, resistant humus, so D–II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Phosphorus cycles through an ecosystem by a sedimentary type of nutrient cycle. What is the reservoir for this type of cycle?","image":"","options":["Atmosphere","Hydrosphere","Biomass of producers","Earth's crust"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Nutrient cycles are of two types: gaseous and sedimentary.\nThe gaseous cycle (for example, carbon) has the atmosphere or hydrosphere as its reservoir.\nThe sedimentary cycle (for example, phosphorus) has the Earth's crust as its reservoir.\nAtmosphere and hydrosphere are therefore the reservoirs of gaseous cycles, not of phosphorus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Some microbes further degrade humus, which releases inorganic nutrients into the soil. This process is known as:","image":"","options":["Catabolism","Mineralisation","Humification","Leaching"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the release of inorganic nutrients from humus by certain microbes as mineralisation.\nHumification is the reverse in effect, because it builds up humus.\nCatabolism is the breakdown of detritus by bacterial and fungal enzymes, not specifically the breakdown of humus.\nLeaching carries water-soluble nutrients down the soil horizon.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a terrestrial ecosystem, through which pathway does a much larger fraction of energy flow?","image":"","options":["Grazing food chain","Through herbivores directly to top carnivores","Detritus food chain","Equally through grazing and detritus food chains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain (DFC) than through the grazing food chain (GFC).\nThe GFC is the major conduit for energy flow only in aquatic ecosystems.\nSo the tempting option 'grazing food chain' describes aquatic ecosystems, not terrestrial ones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Saprotroph\nB. Omnivore\nC. Herbivore of aquatic ecosystem\nD. Producer of terrestrial ecosystem\nList-II\nI. Mollusc\nII. Fungus\nIII. Woody plant\nIV. Crow\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-II: Fungi are decomposers that degrade dead matter, i.e. saprotrophs.\nB-IV: NCERT cites crows and cockroaches as omnivores.\nC-I: Molluscs are given as herbivores in aquatic ecosystems.\nD-III: Herbaceous and woody plants are the major producers on land.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which type of ecological pyramid can never be inverted in any ecosystem?","image":"","options":["Pyramid of numbers","Pyramid of biomass","Pyramid of energy","Both pyramid of numbers and pyramid of biomass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: as energy passes from one trophic level to the next, some of it is always lost as heat.\nSo each higher trophic level always has less energy than the level below it. The pyramid of energy is therefore always upright.\nThe pyramid of numbers can be inverted, as with insects on one big tree.\nThe pyramid of biomass can be inverted, as in the sea.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a pond ecosystem, the decomposers such as fungi, bacteria and flagellates are especially abundant in which region?","image":"","options":["Surface film of the water","Bottom of the pond","Floating leaves of marginal plants","Middle layer of open water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names fungi, bacteria and flagellates as the decomposers of a pond.\nThey are especially abundant at the bottom of the pond.\nDead organic matter settles there and joins the rich soil deposit, so decomposers have the most raw material to work on.\nThe surface and open water are dominated by phytoplankton and zooplankton instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (step of decomposition) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(a) Humification\n(b) Mineralisation\n(c) Catabolism\n(d) Leaching\nList-II:\n(i) Water-soluble inorganic nutrients move down into the soil horizon\n(ii) Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances\n(iii) Release of inorganic nutrients from humus by microbes\n(iv) Accumulation of a dark, amorphous, colloidal substance","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Humification forms humus, a dark, amorphous, colloidal substance, so (a) matches (iv).\n(b) Mineralisation releases inorganic nutrients from humus, so (b) matches (iii).\n(c) Catabolism is the enzymatic degradation of detritus by bacteria and fungi, so (c) matches (ii).\n(d) Leaching carries soluble nutrients down the soil horizon, so (d) matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Major producers of a terrestrial ecosystem\nB. Producers of an aquatic ecosystem\nC. Share of incident solar radiation that is photosynthetically active\nD. Share of PAR actually captured by plants\nList-II\n(i) 2–10 per cent\n(ii) Phytoplankton, algae and higher plants\n(iii) Less than 50 per cent\n(iv) Herbaceous and woody plants","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iv): In terrestrial ecosystems, the major producers are herbaceous and woody plants.\nB-(ii): Aquatic producers include phytoplankton, algae and higher plants.\nC-(iii): Less than 50 per cent of incident solar radiation is PAR.\nD-(i): Plants capture only 2–10 per cent of the PAR, and this small amount sustains the living world.\nOption (2) is tempting because it swaps the two percentages.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The net primary productivity of a forest is always lower than its gross primary productivity.\nReason (R): A considerable amount of the organic matter produced in photosynthesis is used by the plants themselves in respiration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NPP = GPP − R, and plants always respire, so R is greater than 0.\nR is true: NCERT states that a considerable amount of GPP is used by plants in respiration.\nThis respiratory loss is exactly why NPP falls below GPP, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of detritus?","image":"","options":["Fallen dry leaves","Shed bark of a tree","Fecal matter of grazing animals","Living phytoplankton in a pond"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Detritus is the dead organic matter that serves as raw material for decomposition.\nNCERT lists dead leaves, bark, flowers, dead animal remains and fecal matter as detritus.\nOptions (1), (2) and (3) therefore all count as detritus.\nLiving phytoplankton are producers, not dead remains, so they are not detritus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The annual NPP of the biosphere is about 170 billion tons of dry organic matter.\n(ii) Oceans occupy about 70 per cent of the Earth's surface.\n(iii) The total annual NPP of land is less than that of oceans.\n(iv) Primary productivity is the same in all ecosystems because it depends only on sunlight.\n(v) Primary productivity depends on the plant species, nutrient availability and photosynthetic capacity of plants.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT figure.\n(ii) Correct: oceans cover about 70% of the surface.\n(iii) Incorrect: land gives about 115 billion tons, which is more than the oceans' 55 billion tons.\n(iv) Incorrect: productivity varies between ecosystems because it depends on several factors, not sunlight alone.\n(v) Correct: these are the factors listed in NCERT.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Pyramid of numbers in a grassland\nB. Pyramid of biomass in the sea\nC. Pyramid of energy\nD. Standing crop\nList-II:\n(i) A small standing crop of phytoplankton supports a large standing crop of zooplankton\n(ii) The mass of living material at a trophic level at a particular time\n(iii) Nearly 6 million plants support only 3 top carnivores\n(iv) Can never be inverted, because heat is lost at each transfer\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-(iii): The grassland pyramid of numbers is upright; about 6 million plants support only 3 top carnivores.\nB-(i): The sea biomass pyramid is inverted, because a small phytoplankton crop supports a larger zooplankton crop.\nC-(iv): The energy pyramid is always upright, since heat is lost at each step.\nD-(ii): Standing crop is the living mass, or number, at a trophic level at a given time.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as consumers of a pond ecosystem?\nZooplankton, free-swimming forms, bottom-dwelling forms, phytoplankton, submerged plants, fungi","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Consumers: zooplankton, free-swimming forms and bottom-dwelling forms.\nNot consumers:\nPhytoplankton and submerged plants are autotrophs.\nFungi are decomposers.\nThat gives 3 consumers in total.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A considerable amount of gross primary productivity is utilised by plants in respiration.\nStatement II: Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by producers.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Plants use a considerable part of GPP for respiration, which is why NPP is always less than GPP. \nStatement II is incorrect. Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers, not by producers. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Deep-sea hydrothermal ecosystem\n(B) Photosynthetically active radiation\n(C) First law of thermodynamics\n(D) Second law of thermodynamics\nList-II:\n(i) Less than 50% of incident solar radiation\n(ii) Energy source other than the Sun\n(iii) Constant energy input needed to counter increasing disorder\n(iv) Energy is transformed, not created or destroyed, as it passes along a food chain","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-ii: hydrothermal vent ecosystems are the only ones not powered by the Sun.\nB-i: PAR is less than 50% of incident solar radiation.\nC-iv: the first law says energy is conserved, so it is only transformed and transferred along the food chain.\nD-iii: the second law of increasing disorder means ecosystems need a continuous energy input.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following ecosystems is the exception in which the Sun is NOT the source of energy?","image":"","options":["Tropical rain forest","Deep-sea hydrothermal ecosystem","Open ocean surface waters","Desert ecosystem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the Sun is the only source of energy for all ecosystems on Earth, except the deep-sea hydrothermal ecosystem.\nRain forests and deserts are run by sunlight captured by land plants.\nThe open ocean surface is tempting because it is marine, but its phytoplankton still use sunlight. Only the deep-sea hydrothermal vents are independent of the Sun.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. About 170 billion tons\nB. About 55 billion tons\nC. About 70 per cent\nD. GPP − R\nList-II\n(i) Share of Earth's surface covered by oceans\n(ii) Net primary productivity\n(iii) Annual NPP of oceans\n(iv) Annual NPP of the whole biosphere","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: 170 billion tons is the biosphere's annual NPP, so A-(iv).\nB: 55 billion tons is the oceans' annual NPP, so B-(iii).\nC: about 70% is the ocean cover of the Earth's surface, so C-(i).\nD: GPP minus respiration losses gives NPP, so D-(ii).\nOption (2) swaps the biosphere and ocean values.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about ecological pyramids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Any estimate of energy, biomass or numbers at a trophic level must include all the organisms at that level","A trophic level represents a functional level and not a particular species","In an ecosystem based on a single large tree, the pyramid of numbers is upright because producers outnumber herbivores","In most ecosystems, producers exceed herbivores in both number and biomass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT says generalisations fail if only a few individuals at a level are counted.\n(2) Correct: a trophic level is functional. For example, a sparrow is a primary consumer when it eats seeds and a secondary consumer when it eats insects.\n(3) Incorrect: one big tree supports many insects, so producers are fewer in number and the base is narrow. This number pyramid is not upright.\n(4) Correct: in most ecosystems, all the pyramids are upright.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Under the same climatic conditions, which type of detritus would be decomposed most quickly?","image":"","options":["Woody twigs rich in lignin","Insect exoskeletons rich in chitin","Leaf litter rich in nitrogen and water-soluble sugars","A mixture of lignin-rich bark and chitin-rich remains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a given climate, the chemical composition of detritus controls how fast it decomposes.\nDetritus rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances such as sugars decomposes quickly.\nDetritus rich in lignin or chitin decomposes slowly, so options 1, 2 and 4 are slower.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Identifying and listing the plant and animal species of an ecosystem gives its species composition.\nStatement II: In the stratification of a forest, shrubs occupy the topmost vertical layer, and trees form the second layer.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT defines species composition as the identification and enumeration of the plant and animal species of an ecosystem.\nStatement II: Incorrect. In a forest, trees occupy the top stratum. Shrubs form the second layer, and herbs and grasses occupy the bottom layers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During decomposition, some nutrients released from detritus may become unavailable to plants.\nReason (R): In leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients move down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: not all nutrients released from detritus stay available to plant roots.\nR is true: in leaching, water carries soluble inorganic nutrients down the soil horizon, where they precipitate as unavailable salts.\nR directly explains why some nutrients become unavailable, as stated in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The annual net primary productivity of the whole biosphere is approximately 170 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter.\n(ii) Oceans, though covering about 70% of the Earth's surface, contribute only about 55 billion tons of this productivity.\n(iii) Net primary productivity equals gross primary productivity minus respiration losses.\n(iv) Productivity is expressed in units of g m$^{-2}$ without any time component.\n(v) Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT gives about 170 billion tons (dry weight) for the biosphere.\n(ii) Correct: oceans contribute only about 55 billion tons; the rest is produced on land.\n(iii) Correct: GPP − R = NPP.\n(iv) Incorrect: g m$^{-2}$ is the unit of production. Productivity is a rate, expressed as g m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$ or kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\n(v) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of secondary productivity.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about trophic levels and ecological pyramids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Saprophytes are given no place in ecological pyramids even though they play a vital role in the ecosystem","Ecological pyramids easily accommodate food webs because they show a species at every trophic level it occupies","A sparrow acts as a secondary consumer when it eats insects and worms","A trophic level represents a functional level and not a particular species"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT lists the omission of saprophytes as a limitation of pyramids.\n(2) Incorrect: pyramids assume a simple food chain and do not accommodate a food web. They also ignore a species belonging to two or more trophic levels.\n(3) Correct: a sparrow is a secondary consumer when it eats insects or worms, and a primary consumer when it eats seeds.\n(4) Correct: a trophic level is a functional level, and one species may occupy several levels.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about a pond as a model ecosystem:\n(i) Solar input, the cycle of temperature and day-length regulate the rate of function of the entire pond.\n(ii) Floating, submerged and marginal plants found at the edges are part of the autotrophic component.\n(iii) Bottom-dwelling forms in a pond are classified as decomposers rather than consumers.\n(iv) Mineralisation of dead matter releases inorganic nutrients that are reused by autotrophs.\n(v) Heat lost by pond organisms is reabsorbed by phytoplankton, so energy flow in the pond is cyclic.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT states that these climatic factors regulate the rate of function of the pond.\n(ii) Correct: floating, submerged and marginal plants are autotrophs, along with phytoplankton and algae.\n(iii) Incorrect: bottom-dwelling forms are consumers. The decomposers are fungi, bacteria and flagellates.\n(iv) Correct: mineralised nutrients return to the autotrophs and are reused repeatedly.\n(v) Incorrect: energy moves unidirectionally to higher trophic levels and is dissipated as heat. It is not recycled.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a garden compost pit, earthworms feed on fallen leaves and break them into much smaller pieces. Which step of decomposition does this activity represent?","image":"","options":["Catabolism","Fragmentation","Leaching","Mineralisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Detritivores such as earthworms physically break detritus into smaller particles. NCERT calls this step fragmentation.\nIt increases the surface area of the detritus for microbes to act on.\nCatabolism is the most tempting wrong option, but it is enzymatic breakdown by bacteria and fungi, not physical breakdown by detritivores.\nLeaching is the downward movement of soluble nutrients. Mineralisation is the release of inorganic nutrients from humus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Stratification\n(b) Gaseous nutrient cycle\n(c) Sedimentary nutrient cycle\n(d) Ecosystem service\nList-II\n(i) Earth's crust acts as the reservoir\n(ii) Purification of air and water by forests\n(iii) Vertical distribution of species at different levels\n(iv) Atmosphere or hydrosphere acts as the reservoir\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Stratification is the vertical distribution of species, so it matches (iii).\n(b) A gaseous cycle such as carbon has the atmosphere or hydrosphere as its reservoir, so it matches (iv).\n(c) A sedimentary cycle such as phosphorus has the Earth's crust as its reservoir, so it matches (i).\n(d) Purification of air and water by forests is an ecosystem service, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Detritus\n(B) Fragmentation\n(C) Leaching\n(D) Catabolism\nList-II\n(i) Degradation of detritus into simpler inorganic substances by bacterial and fungal enzymes\n(ii) Dead leaves, bark, flowers and animal remains including fecal matter\n(iii) Breaking of detritus into smaller particles by detritivores\n(iv) Downward movement of water-soluble inorganic nutrients into the soil horizon\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Detritus is the dead plant and animal matter, including fecal matter, so A-(ii).\n(B) Fragmentation is the physical breakdown of detritus by detritivores such as earthworms, so B-(iii).\n(C) Leaching carries soluble inorganic nutrients down into the soil, so C-(iv).\n(D) Catabolism is enzymatic degradation by bacteria and fungi, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A primary carnivore is a secondary consumer.\nReason (R): A primary carnivore feeds on animals that in turn feed on plants or their produce.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT calls carnivores that eat herbivores primary carnivores, though they are secondary consumers.\nR is true. Secondary consumers are animals that eat other animals which eat plants.\nR explains A. Because its food is a herbivore, the primary carnivore is one step above the primary consumer, which makes it the secondary consumer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following conditions would slow down the rate of decomposition?\nDetritus rich in lignin; detritus rich in chitin; detritus rich in nitrogen; detritus rich in sugars; low temperature; anaerobiosis; warm and moist environment","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slow decomposition: detritus rich in lignin, detritus rich in chitin, low temperature, and anaerobiosis. Decomposition is largely an oxygen-requiring process, so lack of oxygen inhibits it.\nFast decomposition: detritus rich in nitrogen or in water-soluble substances like sugars, and a warm, moist environment.\nSo 4 conditions slow decomposition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"When a sparrow feeds on insects and worms, it acts as a","image":"","options":["Producer","Primary consumer","Secondary consumer","Decomposer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Insects and worms are themselves consumers.\nAn animal that eats them is therefore a secondary consumer.\nNCERT notes that a sparrow is a primary consumer only when it eats seeds, fruits and peas.\nBecause its role depends on what it eats, a sparrow can occupy more than one trophic level.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A trophic level represents a particular species in an ecosystem.\nStatement II: A given species may occupy more than one trophic level in the same ecosystem at the same time.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT stresses that a trophic level is a functional level, not a species as such.\nStatement II is correct. A sparrow, for example, is a primary consumer when it eats seeds and a secondary consumer when it eats insects.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"A student draws an ecological pyramid in which the bottom bar (producers) is the narrowest. The bars above it, representing consumers, are wider. Which situation does this diagram most likely represent?","image":"","options":["Pyramid of energy in a pond","Pyramid of biomass in a sea, with phytoplankton supporting a larger standing crop of zooplankton","Pyramid of biomass in a grassland","Pyramid of numbers in a grassland"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A narrow producer base with wider consumer bars is an inverted pyramid.\nNCERT shows the sea's biomass pyramid as inverted. A small standing crop of phytoplankton supports a larger standing crop of zooplankton and fishes.\nAn energy pyramid can never be inverted, so option 1 fails.\nGrassland pyramids of biomass and of numbers are upright, so options 3 and 4 fail.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A trophic level represents a functional level, not a particular species.\n(ii) If producers trap 10,000 J of energy, a primary carnivore in that grazing food chain receives about 100 J according to the 10 per cent law.\n(iii) A given species can occupy only one trophic level in an ecosystem at any one time.\n(iv) Standing crop may be expressed as the number of organisms in a unit area.\n(v) In aquatic ecosystems, a higher trophic level can contain more energy than the trophic level below it.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. NCERT stresses that a trophic level is functional, not a species.\n(ii) Correct. The energy passes 10,000 J → 1,000 J (herbivore) → 100 J (primary carnivore).\n(iii) Incorrect. A species can occupy several levels at the same time. Sparrows and humans (omnivores) are examples.\n(iv) Correct. Standing crop is measured as biomass or as number per unit area.\n(v) Incorrect. Energy always decreases at higher levels, so the energy pyramid is never inverted.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pyramid of energy is always upright.\nReason (R): Energy from the top trophic level is recycled back to the producers, so the base always remains the largest.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level, so the energy pyramid is always upright.\nR is false. Energy flow is unidirectional, from the sun to producers to consumers, and it is never recycled.\nThe pyramid is upright because some energy is lost as heat at every transfer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Decomposition of dead organic matter is very slow in waterlogged soils.\nReason (R): Decomposition is largely an oxygen-requiring process.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Waterlogged soil is poorly aerated, which creates anaerobiosis, and anaerobiosis inhibits decomposition.\nR is true. NCERT states that decomposition is largely an oxygen-requiring process.\nDecomposition slows in waterlogged soil precisely because oxygen is lacking, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a pond ecosystem, which group of organisms forms the decomposers and is especially abundant at the bottom of the pond?","image":"","options":["Phytoplankton and submerged plants","Zooplankton and free-swimming forms","Fungi, bacteria and flagellates","Molluscs and bottom-dwelling fishes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the pond's decomposers as fungi, bacteria and flagellates. They are especially abundant at the pond bottom.\nPhytoplankton and submerged plants are autotrophs.\nZooplankton and free-swimming or bottom-dwelling animals are consumers.\nBottom-dwelling forms are tempting because of their location. However, they consume food; they do not decompose detritus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following belongs to the autotrophic component of a pond ecosystem?","image":"","options":["Zooplankton","Bottom-dwelling forms","Phytoplankton","Flagellates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, the autotrophs of a pond are phytoplankton, some algae, and floating, submerged and marginal plants.\nZooplankton and bottom-dwelling forms are consumers.\nNCERT lists flagellates among the decomposers, along with fungi and bacteria.\nSo only phytoplankton makes food by photosynthesis here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"A sparrow eats caterpillars that feed on leaves. While feeding in this way, the sparrow functions as a:","image":"","options":["Primary consumer at the second trophic level","Secondary consumer at the third trophic level","Tertiary consumer at the fourth trophic level","Decomposer feeding on detritus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The chain here is leaves (producer, first level) → caterpillar (herbivore, second level) → sparrow.\nThe sparrow eats an animal that eats plants, so it is a secondary consumer at the third trophic level.\nOption (1) is tempting because a sparrow is a primary consumer when it eats seeds or fruits. That does not apply when it eats insects.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the interconnection of food chains is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Some organisms of the detritus food chain are prey to animals of the grazing food chain.","In a terrestrial ecosystem, more energy flows through the grazing food chain than through the detritus food chain, which is why a food web forms.","Omnivores such as cockroaches and crows help link different food chains.","The natural interconnection of food chains forms a food web."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that some DFC organisms are prey to GFC animals.\n(2) Incorrect: in terrestrial ecosystems, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the DFC. Also, a food web arises from the interconnection of chains, not from GFC dominance.\n(3) Correct: omnivores like cockroaches and crows feed on more than one level.\n(4) Correct: interconnected food chains make a food web.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Oceans cover about 70% of the Earth's surface, yet their productivity is low. Which pair of factors mainly accounts for this?","image":"","options":["Absence of decomposers and excess of oxygen in sea water","Limited light penetration below the surface layers and scarcity of nutrients in sunlit waters","Very high temperature of sea water and lack of carbon dioxide","Excessive grazing by fishes and absence of phytoplankton"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Primary productivity depends on light, nutrient availability and the photosynthetic capacity of producers.\nIn oceans, light reaches only the upper layers, so photosynthesis is limited to a thin zone. That sunlit zone is often poor in nutrients.\nOption (1) is wrong because oceans do have decomposers.\nOption (4) is wrong because phytoplankton are the main marine producers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of aquatic ecosystems?\nPond, desert, estuary, wetland, grassland, river, forest, lake","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Aquatic ecosystems: pond, estuary, wetland, river and lake. That makes 5.\nTerrestrial ecosystems: desert, grassland and forest.\nNCERT lists forest, grassland and desert as terrestrial examples, and pond, lake, wetland, river and estuary as aquatic examples.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pyramid of biomass in the sea is generally inverted.\nReason (R): In the sea, the biomass of phytoplankton far exceeds the biomass of fishes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the pyramid of biomass in the sea is generally inverted.\nR is false. It is the reverse: the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.\nA small standing crop of phytoplankton supports a larger standing crop of consumers. This is what makes the pyramid inverted.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a forest, different plant species occupy different vertical levels. Trees form the top layer and herbs and grasses form the bottom layers. What is this vertical distribution of species called?","image":"","options":["Species composition","Stratification","Ecological succession","Trophic organisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines stratification as the vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels.\nIn a forest, trees occupy the top stratum, shrubs the second, and herbs and grasses the bottom layers.\nSpecies composition is tempting but wrong. It refers to identifying and listing the species present, not their vertical arrangement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about decomposition are correct?\n(i) Decomposers break complex organic matter into carbon dioxide, water and nutrients.\n(ii) Fecal matter of animals forms part of detritus.\n(iii) Leaching makes nutrients more readily available to plant roots in the topsoil.\n(iv) Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances.\n(v) Fragmentation is the enzymatic conversion of detritus into inorganic ions.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Judge each statement:\n(i) Correct: these are the end products of decomposition.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT includes fecal matter in detritus.\n(iii) Incorrect: leaching carries nutrients down, where they are precipitated as unavailable salts.\n(iv) Correct: this is the definition of catabolism.\n(v) Incorrect: fragmentation is the physical breakdown of detritus into smaller particles by detritivores, not an enzymatic conversion.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"During leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients:","image":"","options":["move up to the soil surface and are absorbed immediately by roots","go down into the soil horizon and get precipitated as unavailable salts","are converted into humus by bacterial enzymes","are released into the atmosphere as gases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes leaching as follows: water-soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil horizon.\nThere they get precipitated as unavailable salts.\nThey do not move upward or become immediately available to roots, so option (1) is wrong.\nHumus formation is humification, not leaching, so option (3) is wrong.\nLoss of nutrients as gases is not part of leaching, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Pyramid of numbers in a grassland\n(b) Pyramid of numbers for a big tree with insects, small birds and larger birds\n(c) Pyramid of biomass in the sea\n(d) Pyramid of energy\nList-II\n(i) Always upright, since energy is lost as heat at each step\n(ii) Narrow base, because a single producer supports many herbivores\n(iii) Upright, with millions of producers at the broad base\n(iv) Inverted, because a small standing crop of producers supports a larger consumer biomass","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): A grassland has about 6 million plants at the base, so its number pyramid is upright.\n(b)-(ii): One tree supports many insects, so the base is narrower than the level above it.\n(c)-(iv): In the sea, a small phytoplankton crop supports a larger consumer biomass.\n(d)-(i): Heat is lost at every transfer, so the energy pyramid is always upright.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An ecosystem needs a constant supply of energy to function and sustain itself.\nReason (R): Ecosystems are not exempt from the second law of thermodynamics. They must spend energy to synthesise the molecules they need and to counteract the universal tendency toward increasing disorderliness.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a constant input of solar energy is the basic requirement for any ecosystem to function and sustain.\nR is true: NCERT states that ecosystems obey the second law of thermodynamics and need energy to build molecules against increasing disorder.\nR explains A. Energy keeps being used up to maintain order and is lost as heat, so it must be supplied continuously.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a grassland, the gross primary productivity is 25000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$. The producers use 60% of this in respiration. What is the net primary productivity?","image":"","options":["15000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","10000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","40000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$","2500 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: GPP = 25000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$; respiration R = 60% of GPP. \nFormula: NPP = GPP − R. \nSubstituting: R = 0.6 × 25000 = 15000, so NPP = 25000 − 15000. \nSo NPP = 10000 kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$. \nOption (1) is the respiration loss, not the NPP. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In decomposition, fragmentation must be fully completed before leaching and catabolism can begin on the detritus.\nStatement II: Catabolism involves enzymes of bacteria and fungi that degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT stresses that fragmentation, leaching and catabolism all operate simultaneously on the detritus. They do not follow a strict sequence.\nStatement II is correct. Catabolism is the enzymatic breakdown of detritus into simpler inorganic substances by bacterial and fungal enzymes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In any plant community, NPP is less than GPP.\n(ii) Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.\n(iii) Oceans contribute more than half of the annual net primary productivity of the biosphere.\n(iv) Primary productivity depends on the availability of nutrients and the photosynthetic capacity of plants.\n(v) Primary production can be expressed in terms of energy as kcal m$^{-2}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NPP = GPP − R, and plants always lose some organic matter in respiration.\n(ii) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of secondary productivity.\n(iii) Incorrect: the oceans contribute about 55 of 170 billion tons, which is roughly one-third.\n(iv) Correct: both are factors listed by NCERT.\n(v) Correct: primary production is expressed as weight (g m$^{-2}$) or energy (kcal m$^{-2}$).\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"According to the ideal pyramid of energy shown in NCERT, roughly what percentage of the sunlight energy available to primary producers is converted into net primary productivity (NPP)?","image":"","options":["10%","1%","50%","20%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT Fig. 12.4(d) notes that primary producers convert only about 1% of the available sunlight energy into NPP.\n10% is the tempting wrong choice. That figure is the transfer efficiency between successive trophic levels (the 10 per cent law), not the conversion of sunlight into NPP.\nLess than 50% of incident radiation is PAR.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the annual net primary productivity of the whole biosphere is approximately:","image":"","options":["55 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter","170 billion tons (dry weight) of organic matter","170 billion tons (fresh weight) of organic matter","115 billion tons (fresh weight) of organic matter"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the annual NPP of the entire biosphere as about 170 billion tons of organic matter.\nThe figure is stated on a dry weight basis, because dry weight is the more accurate way to measure biomass.\n55 billion tons is only the oceans' share, so option (1) is wrong.\nOptions (3) and (4) are wrong because they use fresh weight.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In the grazing food chain Grass → Goat → Man, man occupies the position of a","image":"","options":["secondary consumer","primary consumer","tertiary consumer","producer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Grass is the producer and is at the first trophic level.\nThe goat eats grass, so it is the primary consumer.\nMan eats the goat, an animal that eats plants, so man is the secondary consumer here.\nThe tertiary consumer option is wrong because there is no further level in this chain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a pond, the same inorganic nutrients can be used again and again by the autotrophs.\nReason (R): Decomposers carry out decomposition and mineralisation of dead matter, releasing inorganic substances back into the water and soil.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says the conversion, consumption and decomposition events in a pond are repeated over and over again, so nutrients are reused.\nR is true. Decomposers at the bottom mineralise dead matter and return inorganic nutrients for reuse by the autotrophs.\nR explains A, because mineralisation is exactly what makes nutrient reuse possible.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about humification, mineralisation and the rate of decomposition are correct?\n(i) Humus is dark coloured and amorphous, and it decomposes at an extremely slow rate.\n(ii) Under the same climate, nitrogen-rich detritus decomposes faster than lignin-rich detritus.\n(iii) Temperature and soil moisture regulate decomposition mainly through their effect on the activity of soil microbes.\n(iv) Leaching is the process by which humus is converted into inorganic nutrients.\n(v) Anaerobic conditions in waterlogged soil speed up decomposition.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: humus is dark, amorphous and resistant to microbial action, so it decomposes extremely slowly.\n(ii) Correct: nitrogen and sugars favour fast decomposition, while lignin and chitin slow it.\n(iii) Correct: these climatic factors act by affecting soil microbe activity.\n(iv) Incorrect: humus is converted into inorganic nutrients by mineralisation. Leaching only moves soluble nutrients down the soil, where they precipitate.\n(v) Incorrect: anaerobiosis inhibits decomposition, so organic matter builds up.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about food chains and food webs are correct?\n(i) The detritus food chain begins with dead organic matter.\n(ii) Some organisms of the detritus food chain are prey for animals of the grazing food chain.\n(iii) The death of an organism is the beginning of the detritus food chain.\n(iv) Decomposers first engulf dead matter and then digest it inside their bodies.\n(v) Omnivores such as crows help link separate food chains into a food web.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the detritus food chain starts from detritus, i.e. dead organic matter.\n(ii) Correct: this is one way the detritus and grazing chains connect.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT states that death begins the detritus food chain or web.\n(iv) Incorrect: decomposers secrete enzymes outside their bodies first, then absorb the simple products.\n(v) Correct: omnivores such as crows and cockroaches interconnect chains into a food web.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A sparrow acts as a primary consumer when it eats seeds and as a secondary consumer when it eats insects.\nStatement II: A trophic level represents a functional level and not a species as such.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Eating seeds, fruits or peas makes the sparrow a primary consumer, and eating insects or worms makes it a secondary consumer.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT stresses that a trophic level is a functional level, so one species can occupy more than one level at the same time.\nThis is also why pyramids are limited: they cannot show a species at two levels.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly shows the direction of energy flow in an ecosystem?","image":"","options":["Sun → Producers → Consumers, never returning to the Sun","Consumers → Producers → Sun, in a closed cycle","Sun → Consumers → Producers","Producers → Sun → Consumers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"All organisms depend on producers for food, either directly or indirectly.\nSo energy moves from the Sun to producers and then to consumers.\nThis flow is unidirectional: energy is lost as heat at each step and is not cycled back.\nA closed cycle describes nutrients, not energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about energy flow in ecosystems are correct?\n(i) Less than 50 per cent of incident solar radiation is photosynthetically active radiation (PAR).\n(ii) Plants capture only 2–10 per cent of the PAR.\n(iii) The sun is the only source of energy for every ecosystem on Earth, including deep-sea hydrothermal ecosystems.\n(iv) Energy flows unidirectionally from the sun to producers and then to consumers.\n(v) In an ideal pyramid of energy, primary producers convert about 10 per cent of the sunlight available to them into NPP.\n(vi) Ecosystems need a constant supply of energy to counteract the tendency towards increasing disorderliness.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: PAR is less than 50% of incident radiation.\n(ii) Correct: plants capture 2–10% of PAR.\n(iii) Incorrect: deep-sea hydrothermal ecosystems are the stated exception.\n(iv) Correct: energy flow is unidirectional.\n(v) Incorrect: producers convert only about 1% of available sunlight into NPP. The 10% figure is the transfer between trophic levels.\n(vi) Correct: this follows from the second law of thermodynamics.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Except for one type of ecosystem, the sun is the only source of energy for all ecosystems on Earth. Which ecosystem is this exception?","image":"","options":["Tropical rainforest ecosystem","Deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem","Estuarine ecosystem","Aquarium (man-made) ecosystem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the sun is the only energy source for all ecosystems on Earth except the deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem.\nSunlight does not reach the deep sea, so these communities depend on energy from hydrothermal vents, not solar radiation.\nA rainforest, an estuary and an aquarium all receive light, and their producers photosynthesise.\nAn aquarium may be man-made, but it still depends on light energy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of ecological pyramids as given in NCERT?\n(i) They do not take into account the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels.\n(ii) They assume a simple food chain.\n(iii) They do not accommodate a food web.\n(iv) Saprophytes are not given any place in them.\n(v) They cannot express energy relationships between trophic levels.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a species occupying several trophic levels is not accounted for.\n(ii) Correct: pyramids assume a simple food chain, which almost never exists in nature.\n(iii) Correct: food webs are not accommodated.\n(iv) Correct: saprophytes are ignored even though they play a vital role.\n(v) Incorrect: the pyramid of energy expresses exactly this relationship.\nSo four statements are limitations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Earthworms are often called the 'farmer's friend'. According to NCERT, which pair of activities earns them this name?","image":"","options":["Fixing atmospheric nitrogen and secreting growth hormones into the soil","Breaking down complex organic matter and loosening the soil","Producing humus directly by photosynthesis and aerating water bodies","Killing soil pathogens and precipitating nutrients as salts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives two reasons for calling earthworms the farmer's friend.\nFirst, they help break down complex organic matter.\nSecond, they loosen the soil.\nThey do not fix nitrogen or photosynthesise, so options (1) and (3) are wrong.\nPrecipitating nutrients as unavailable salts is a result of leaching, not earthworm activity, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In the pyramid of numbers of a grassland ecosystem described in NCERT, nearly 6 million plants support how many top carnivores?","image":"","options":["30","300","3000","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT Fig. 12.4(a) shows a grassland pyramid of numbers.\nThe producer base has nearly 6 million plants. The apex has only 3 top carnivores.\nThe numbers fall sharply at each step because energy decreases at successive trophic levels.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"A small pond is used as a model to understand the structure and function of an ecosystem. Which of the following statements about it is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The abiotic component includes water with dissolved inorganic and organic substances and the soil deposit at the bottom of the pond","Fungi, bacteria and flagellates act as decomposers and are especially abundant at the bottom of the pond","Solar input, the cycle of temperature and day-length regulate the rate of function of the entire pond","Energy released when dead matter is mineralised is returned to the autotrophs and reused, just like the nutrients"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. NCERT includes water, dissolved substances and the bottom soil deposit in the abiotic component.\n(2) Correct. Fungi, bacteria and flagellates are the decomposers and are abundant at the pond bottom.\n(3) Correct. Climatic factors such as solar input, temperature and day-length regulate the pond's functioning.\n(4) Incorrect. Mineralisation returns nutrients for reuse, but energy moves in one direction to higher trophic levels and is lost as heat. Energy is never recycled.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Under the same warm and moist climatic conditions, detritus rich in lignin and chitin decomposes more slowly than detritus rich in sugars.\nReason (R): Decomposition is largely an oxygen-requiring process.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In a given climate, the decomposition rate depends on the chemical composition of detritus. It is slow for lignin- and chitin-rich detritus and quick for detritus rich in nitrogen and sugars.\nR is true. NCERT states that decomposition is largely oxygen-requiring.\nHowever, oxygen need applies equally to both kinds of detritus, so it cannot explain the difference in A.\nThe difference arises from chemical composition, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a grazing food chain, the producers fix 20,000 J of energy. According to the 10 per cent law, how much energy will be available to the secondary consumers?","image":"","options":["2000 J","200 J","20 J","1000 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: energy at the producer level (first trophic level) is 20,000 J.\nFormula: only 10% of the energy passes to each successive trophic level.\nSubstituting: primary consumers receive 0.1 × 20,000 = 2000 J.\nSecondary consumers receive 0.1 × 2000 = 200 J.\nSo the energy available to secondary consumers is 200 J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes humus formed during decomposition?","image":"","options":["A light coloured crystalline substance that is decomposed rapidly by soil bacteria","A dark coloured amorphous colloidal substance that is highly resistant to microbial action and acts as a reservoir of nutrients","Water-soluble inorganic nutrients that move down the soil horizon and precipitate as unavailable salts","Small particles of detritus produced by earthworms during fragmentation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: humification produces humus. Humus is dark coloured and amorphous. It is highly resistant to microbial action and decomposes extremely slowly. Being colloidal, it serves as a reservoir of nutrients.\nOption (1) is wrong because humus is dark, not light, and it decomposes slowly, not rapidly.\nOption (3) describes the products of leaching.\nOption (4) describes the products of fragmentation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism in a grazing food chain) with List-II (trophic level).\nList-I:\n(A) Grass\n(B) Goat feeding on grass\n(C) Man eating goat meat\n(D) Secondary carnivore\nList-II:\n(I) Third trophic level\n(II) Fourth trophic level\n(III) First trophic level\n(IV) Second trophic level","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Grass is a producer, so it is at the first trophic level (III).\n(B) A goat eating grass is a herbivore at the second level (IV).\n(C) Man eating the goat is a secondary consumer at the third level (I).\n(D) A secondary carnivore feeds on primary carnivores, so it is at the fourth level (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Consumers that feed on herbivores are more correctly called","image":"","options":["secondary carnivores","primary carnivores","primary consumers","tertiary consumers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Herbivores are primary consumers.\nAnimals that feed on herbivores are carnivores, more correctly called primary carnivores, even though they are secondary consumers.\nSecondary carnivores feed on primary carnivores, so that option is one level too high.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Under the same climatic conditions, detritus rich in lignin and chitin decomposes more slowly than detritus rich in nitrogen and sugars.\nReason (R): Low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit decomposition, leading to a build-up of organic matter.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Within the same climate, the chemical composition of detritus decides the rate. Lignin and chitin slow decomposition, while nitrogen and water-soluble sugars speed it up.\nR is true. Low temperature and lack of oxygen inhibit microbial activity.\nHowever, A compares detritus under the same climate, so a climatic factor (R) cannot explain it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An ecosystem requires a constant supply of energy to remain functional.\nReason (R): Ecosystems obey the second law of thermodynamics and must synthesise required molecules against the universal tendency towards increasing disorderliness.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: an ecosystem needs a continuous input of energy to function and sustain itself.\nR is true: NCERT states that ecosystems are not exempt from the second law of thermodynamics.\nThey need energy to build molecules and to counteract the universal tendency towards increasing disorderliness.\nThis need is exactly why the energy supply must be constant, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For study, the biosphere is conveniently divided into two basic categories, terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems.\nReason (R): The biosphere, being a composite of all local ecosystems on Earth, is too big and complex to be studied at one time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT divides the global ecosystem into terrestrial and aquatic categories.\nR is true. The biosphere is the composite of all local ecosystems and is too large and complex to study at once.\nR is exactly the reason NCERT gives for the division, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match the components of a pond ecosystem in List-I with their category in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Phytoplankton\nB. Zooplankton\nC. Flagellates\nD. Rich soil deposit at the bottom of the pond\nList-II\n(i) Abiotic component\n(ii) Decomposer\n(iii) Consumer\n(iv) Autotroph","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Phytoplankton are autotrophs because they photosynthesise. A-(iv)\nB. Zooplankton are consumers. A common error is to treat them as producers. B-(iii)\nC. Flagellates are decomposers, along with fungi and bacteria. They are abundant at the pond bottom. C-(ii)\nD. The bottom soil deposit is abiotic, along with water and its dissolved substances. D-(i)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pyramid of biomass in the sea is generally inverted.\nReason (R): At any given time, the standing crop of phytoplankton in the sea is larger than that of the consumers feeding on it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The sea usually has an inverted biomass pyramid.\nR is false. NCERT shows a small standing crop of phytoplankton supporting a large standing crop of zooplankton.\nAlso, the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.\nIf R were true, the pyramid would be upright, not inverted.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair consists only of ecosystems that NCERT treats as man-made?","image":"","options":["Crop field and aquarium","Forest and crop field","Aquarium and lake","Wetland and grassland"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT says crop fields and an aquarium may be considered man-made ecosystems.\nForest and grassland are natural terrestrial ecosystems.\nLake and wetland are natural aquatic ecosystems.\nSo only option (1) has both members man-made.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Secondary productivity is best defined as the","image":"","options":["rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis","biomass left after respiratory losses of producers","rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers","rate at which decomposers convert detritus into inorganic nutrients"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines secondary productivity as the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.\nThe rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis is GPP.\nThe biomass left after the producers' respiratory losses is NPP.\nThe conversion of detritus into inorganic nutrients by decomposers is decomposition, not productivity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Primary production\n(b) Gross primary productivity\n(c) Net primary productivity\n(d) Secondary productivity\nList-II\n(i) Rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers\n(ii) GPP minus respiration losses\n(iii) Biomass produced per unit area over a time period, expressed in g $m^{-2}$\n(iv) Rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): Primary production is the amount of biomass per unit area, in g $m^{-2}$. \n(b)-(iv): GPP is the rate of producing organic matter during photosynthesis. \n(c)-(ii): NPP = GPP − R. \n(d)-(i): Secondary productivity is the rate of new organic matter formed by consumers. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding productivity is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Net primary productivity equals gross primary productivity minus respiration losses","Net primary productivity is the biomass available for consumption by herbivores and decomposers","Productivity is expressed in units of g m$^{-2}$ or kcal m$^{-2}$ only, without any time component","Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option 1: Correct. GPP – R = NPP.\nOption 2: Correct. NPP is what is available to heterotrophs, i.e. herbivores and decomposers.\nOption 3: Incorrect. g m$^{-2}$ and kcal m$^{-2}$ are units of primary production. Productivity is a rate, so it is expressed per unit time, e.g. g m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\nOption 4: Correct. This is the NCERT definition of secondary productivity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under the same climatic conditions, detritus rich in lignin and chitin decomposes faster than detritus rich in nitrogen and sugars.\nStatement II: Low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit decomposition, resulting in a build-up of organic materials.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Detritus rich in lignin and chitin decomposes more slowly. Detritus rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances such as sugars decomposes more quickly.\nStatement II is correct. Decomposition is largely an oxygen-requiring process. Warm and moist conditions favour it, while low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit it, so organic matter accumulates.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"The annual NPP of the biosphere is about 170 billion tons (dry weight), of which oceans contribute 55 billion tons. Oceans cover about 70% of Earth's surface and land covers the remaining 30%. How many times greater is the average NPP per unit surface area on land than in the oceans (approximately)?","image":"","options":["2.1","4.9","0.48","1.6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: total NPP = 170, ocean NPP = 55 (billion tons); ocean area = 70%, land area = 30%. \nLand NPP = 170 − 55 = 115 billion tons. \nNPP per unit area: land = 115/30 ≈ 3.83; ocean = 55/70 ≈ 0.786. \nRatio = 3.83 / 0.786 ≈ 4.9. \nSo land is about 4.9 times more productive per unit area than the oceans. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a field study, equal amounts of dead leaves were placed in two nylon bags on moist forest soil. Bag X had very fine mesh that kept out earthworms but allowed bacteria and fungi in. Bag Y had coarse mesh that allowed earthworms in as well. After some months, the leaves in bag X were noticeably less broken into small pieces. Which step of decomposition was mainly restricted in bag X?","image":"","options":["Catabolism, because microbes could not reach the leaves","Leaching, because the fine mesh blocked water movement","Fragmentation, because detritivores were excluded","Humification, because sunlight could not reach the leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fragmentation is the breaking of detritus into small particles by detritivores such as earthworms.\nThe fine mesh in bag X kept out earthworms, so fragmentation was mainly restricted there.\nBacteria and fungi could still enter bag X, so catabolism was not blocked. This rules out option (1).\nWater can pass through mesh, so leaching was still possible. This rules out option (2).\nHumification does not depend on sunlight. This rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the functioning of a pond ecosystem is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Autotrophs convert inorganic materials into organic materials using the radiant energy of the sun","Heterotrophs obtain their food by consuming autotrophs directly or indirectly","Heat energy dissipated by consumers is reabsorbed by phytoplankton and used again for photosynthesis","Decomposition and mineralisation release materials from dead matter for reuse by autotrophs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct. Autotrophs fix solar radiant energy into organic matter.\nOption (2) is correct. Heterotrophs consume the autotrophs.\nOption (3) is incorrect. Energy moves unidirectionally towards higher trophic levels and is lost as heat. This heat cannot be recaptured for photosynthesis. Only materials are recycled, not energy.\nOption (4) is correct. Mineralisation returns nutrients to the autotrophs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose all the fungi, bacteria and flagellates at the bottom of a pond were suddenly eliminated, while sunlight, temperature and day-length remained normal. What is the most likely long-term consequence?","image":"","options":["Energy would begin to flow from consumers back to the producers","Dead organic matter would accumulate and the productivity of phytoplankton would decline as mineral nutrients are not regenerated","Zooplankton would start photosynthesising to replace the lost producers","Solar input to the pond would decrease, lowering the temperature of the water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fungi, bacteria and flagellates are the pond's decomposers. They mineralise dead matter and return inorganic nutrients to the autotrophs.\nWithout them, detritus piles up and nutrients stay locked in organic form.\nPhytoplankton gradually run short of nutrients, so their productivity falls.\nEnergy flow remains unidirectional, zooplankton cannot photosynthesise, and solar input does not depend on decomposers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Use the NCERT data: annual NPP of the biosphere is about 170 billion tons, oceans contribute about 55 billion tons, and oceans cover about 70% of the Earth's surface (land covers the rest). Approximately how many times greater is the average NPP per unit area on land than in the oceans?","image":"","options":["About 2 times","About 3 times","About 5 times","About 10 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Land NPP = 170 − 55 = 115 billion tons, produced on 30% of the surface.\nLand NPP per unit share of area = 115/30 ≈ 3.83.\nOcean NPP per unit share of area = 55/70 ≈ 0.79.\nRatio = 3.83/0.79 ≈ 4.9, which is about 5 times.\nOption (2) comes from comparing only the totals (115/55 ≈ 2) and ignoring area.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes fragmentation from catabolism in decomposition?","image":"","options":["Fragmentation reduces detritus to smaller particles without converting it into inorganic substances, whereas catabolism uses bacterial and fungal enzymes to degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances","Fragmentation is carried out by fungal enzymes in the soil horizon, whereas catabolism is carried out by earthworms feeding on litter","Fragmentation must be completed before catabolism starts, whereas leaching occurs only after catabolism ends","Fragmentation produces unavailable salts in the soil horizon, whereas catabolism produces dark, amorphous humus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Fragmentation is the physical breakdown of detritus into smaller particles by detritivores.\nCatabolism is the enzymatic degradation of detritus into simpler inorganic substances by bacteria and fungi.\nOption (2) swaps the agents of the two steps.\nOption (3) imposes a strict sequence, but NCERT says the steps operate simultaneously.\nOption (4) confuses fragmentation with leaching and catabolism with humification.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Many ecologists regard the entire biosphere as a global ecosystem. This is because the biosphere is:","image":"","options":["The largest terrestrial ecosystem, excluding the oceans","A composite of all the local ecosystems on Earth","The sum of all aquatic ecosystems only","A single population spread over the whole Earth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the biosphere as a composite of all local ecosystems on Earth. It therefore includes both terrestrial and aquatic systems.\nIt is not limited to land (option 1) or to water (option 3).\nIt contains countless species, so it is not a single population (option 4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, net primary productivity represents the biomass available for consumption by","image":"","options":["producers only, for their own respiration","heterotrophs, i.e., herbivores and decomposers","top carnivores only","autotrophs of the next generation only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NPP is what remains of GPP after the plants' own respiration has been met.\nNCERT states that NPP is the biomass available for consumption by heterotrophs, namely herbivores and decomposers.\nThe producers' respiration is already subtracted (R), so option 1 describes GPP use, not NPP.\nTop carnivores get energy only indirectly, through the lower trophic levels.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Heterotrophic organisms of the detritus food chain secrete digestive enzymes onto dead and waste matter. They then absorb the simple inorganic materials released. What are these organisms called?","image":"","options":["Detritivores","Saprotrophs","Autotrophs","Omnivores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes decomposers, mainly fungi and bacteria, as saprotrophs (sapro: to decompose).\nThey digest dead organic matter outside their bodies using secreted enzymes, then absorb the products.\nDetritivores such as earthworms only fragment detritus into smaller particles. They do not digest it externally by secreted enzymes.\nAutotrophs make their own food, and omnivores eat both plants and animals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about trophic levels and ecological pyramids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A trophic level represents a functional level, not a particular species","A sparrow is a primary consumer when it eats seeds and a secondary consumer when it eats insects","Ecological pyramids readily accommodate food webs and give a proper place to saprophytes","Measurement of biomass as dry weight is more accurate than fresh weight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT defines a trophic level as a functional level, not a species.\n(2) Correct: the sparrow occupies two trophic levels depending on what it eats.\n(3) Incorrect: pyramids assume a simple food chain and cannot accommodate a food web. Saprophytes are given no place in them. Both points are listed as limitations of pyramids.\n(4) Correct: dry weight avoids variation caused by water content.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Primary productivity is not the same in all types of ecosystems.\nReason (R): Primary productivity depends on the plant species of an area, environmental factors, availability of nutrients and the photosynthetic capacity of plants.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Primary productivity varies between ecosystems such as forests, deserts and oceans. \nR is true. NCERT lists plant species, environmental factors, nutrient availability and photosynthetic capacity as the factors that determine primary productivity. \nThese factors differ from one ecosystem to another, so R correctly explains A. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Primary production\nB. Productivity\nC. Gross primary productivity\nD. Net primary productivity\nList-II\n(i) Rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis\n(ii) GPP minus respiration losses\n(iii) Amount of biomass produced per unit area over a time period, expressed in g m$^{-2}$\n(iv) Rate of biomass production, expressed in g m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Primary production is an amount of biomass per unit area, expressed in g m$^{-2}$ or kcal m$^{-2}$.\nB-(iv): Productivity is a rate, expressed in g m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\nC-(i): GPP is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis.\nD-(ii): NPP = GPP − R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pyramid of energy is always upright and can never be inverted.\nReason (R): When energy flows from one trophic level to the next, some energy is always lost as heat at each step.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the energy pyramid is always upright.\nR is true. Some energy is dissipated as heat at every transfer between trophic levels.\nBecause of this loss, each higher level has less energy than the level below it. R therefore directly explains why the pyramid cannot be inverted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a pond ecosystem, the producers use 60% of their gross primary productivity in respiration. If the net primary productivity is 6,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$, what is the gross primary productivity?","image":"","options":["3,600 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","9,600 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","10,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$","15,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.6 × GPP and NPP = 6,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\nFormula: NPP = GPP − R = GPP − 0.6 GPP = 0.4 GPP.\nSubstituting: GPP = 6,000 / 0.4.\nSo GPP = 15,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ yr$^{-1}$.\nCheck: R = 9,000, and 15,000 − 9,000 = 6,000.\nThe value 9,600 comes from wrongly adding 60% of NPP to NPP.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of ecological pyramids, as given in NCERT?\n(i) They do not take into account a species belonging to two or more trophic levels.\n(ii) They assume a simple food chain.\n(iii) They do not accommodate a food web.\n(iv) Saprophytes are given no place in them.\n(v) They cannot be constructed for aquatic ecosystems.\n(vi) The pyramid of energy becomes inverted in the sea.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: species that work at two or more levels are ignored.\n(ii) Correct: pyramids assume a simple food chain, which almost never exists in nature.\n(iii) Correct: they do not accommodate a food web.\n(iv) Correct: saprophytes get no place, despite their vital role.\n(v) Wrong: aquatic pyramids are drawn, for example the sea biomass pyramid.\n(vi) Wrong: the energy pyramid is never inverted.\nSo 4 are limitations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Humus acts as a reservoir of nutrients in soil mainly because it is","image":"","options":["colloidal in nature","rapidly degraded by soil bacteria","highly water-soluble and leaches easily","a light-coloured crystalline substance"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes humus as a dark coloured, amorphous substance. It is highly resistant to microbial action and decomposes extremely slowly.\nBecause it is colloidal in nature, it holds nutrients and serves as a nutrient reservoir.\nOption (2) is wrong because humus resists microbes and is degraded very slowly.\nOptions (3) and (4) contradict its described nature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Saprophytes are given no place in ecological pyramids even though they play a vital role in the ecosystem.\n(ii) A pyramid of numbers drawn for insects feeding on a single big tree, and the birds depending on them, would be upright.\n(iii) The deep sea hydrothermal ecosystem is an exception to the sun being the only source of energy for ecosystems.\n(iv) Measuring biomass as fresh weight is more accurate than measuring it as dry weight.\n(v) Humus is colloidal, highly resistant to microbial action and serves as a reservoir of nutrients.\n(vi) In a terrestrial ecosystem, a much larger fraction of energy flows through the detritus food chain than through the grazing food chain.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This is a stated limitation of ecological pyramids.\n(ii) Incorrect. One tree supports many insects, so the base is narrow and the pyramid is not upright.\n(iii) Correct. NCERT names this as the one exception.\n(iv) Incorrect. Dry weight is more accurate, because water content varies.\n(v) Correct. This describes humus as given in NCERT.\n(vi) Correct. In terrestrial ecosystems the detritus food chain carries more energy; in aquatic ones the grazing food chain dominates.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In the grazing food chain Grass → Goat → Man, what does man represent?","image":"","options":["Producer","Primary consumer","Secondary consumer","Tertiary consumer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Grass is the producer. It traps solar energy and occupies the first trophic level.\nThe goat feeds directly on grass, so it is the primary consumer, a herbivore.\nMan eats the goat, an animal that eats plants, so man is the secondary consumer.\nA tertiary consumer would need one more link, an animal feeding on a secondary consumer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In any ecological pyramid, the base represents:","image":"","options":["Producers, the first trophic level","Decomposers","Primary consumers","Top carnivores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The base of every ecological pyramid (number, biomass or energy) is the first trophic level, the producers.\nThe apex is the tertiary or top-level consumer.\nDecomposers (saprophytes) are not given any place in ecological pyramids. This is one of the pyramids' limitations.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Cockroach\n(B) Goat\n(C) Grass\n(D) Bacteria\nList-II:\n(I) Producer\n(II) Saprotroph\n(III) Omnivore\n(IV) Primary consumer","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cockroach: NCERT cites it, with the crow, as an omnivore that links food chains. So A-III.\nGoat: it feeds on grass, so it is a primary consumer. So B-IV.\nGrass: it is the producer. So C-I.\nBacteria: they are decomposers, i.e. saprotrophs. So D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The phosphorus cycle is classified as a sedimentary type of nutrient cycle.\nReason (R): Through nutrient cycling, nutrient elements are stored and moved through the components of an ecosystem and are used repeatedly.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Phosphorus has its reservoir in the Earth's crust, so its cycle is sedimentary.\nR is true. NCERT defines nutrient cycling as the storage and movement of nutrients through ecosystem components, with nutrients reused repeatedly.\nR does not explain A, however. A cycle is called sedimentary because of where its reservoir lies (the Earth's crust), not because nutrients are reused. Reuse is common to all cycles, including gaseous ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Fragmentation, leaching and catabolism all operate on detritus at the same time.\nReason (R): Catabolism by microbial enzymes can begin only after leaching of the detritus is fully completed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assertion: NCERT stresses that all the steps of decomposition operate simultaneously on the detritus. So A is true.\nReason: R claims the steps follow a strict order, with catabolism waiting for leaching to finish. This contradicts the simultaneous operation described in NCERT, so R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In an ecosystem, the term 'standing crop' refers to:","image":"","options":["The mass of living material present at a trophic level at a particular time","The rate at which producers form new organic matter during photosynthesis","The total energy that flows through a trophic level in one year","The mass of dead plant and animal remains on the soil surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines standing crop as the mass of living material present at each trophic level at a particular time.\nIt is measured either as biomass or as the number of organisms per unit area.\nOption (2) describes productivity, which is a rate. Standing crop is not a rate.\nOption (4) describes detritus, which is dead matter.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"A student studies the leaf litter on a moist forest floor and records three observations:\n(P) Millipedes and earthworms chew fallen leaves into tiny bits.\n(Q) After heavy rain, soluble nutrients from the litter move to lower soil layers and precipitate as salts that roots cannot use.\n(R) Fungal hyphae and bacteria secrete enzymes that convert the leaf remains into simpler inorganic substances.\nWhich option correctly identifies P, Q and R and describes how they occur?","image":"","options":["P – catabolism, Q – leaching, R – fragmentation; they occur strictly one after another","P – fragmentation, Q – mineralisation, R – catabolism; they occur simultaneously on the detritus","P – fragmentation, Q – leaching, R – catabolism; they occur strictly in the order P, then Q, then R","P – fragmentation, Q – leaching, R – catabolism; they operate simultaneously on the detritus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"P: Detritivores physically breaking litter into smaller particles is fragmentation.\nQ: Soluble inorganic nutrients moving down the soil horizon and precipitating as unavailable salts is leaching. It is not mineralisation, which releases nutrients from humus.\nR: Enzymatic degradation by fungi and bacteria is catabolism.\nNCERT states that these steps operate simultaneously on the detritus, so option (3) is wrong despite correct labels.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the grazing food chain: grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → hawk. If the hawk receives 2 kJ of energy, how much energy must be present at the frog's trophic level according to the 10 per cent law?","image":"","options":["20 kJ","200 kJ","2000 kJ","20000 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the hawk is at the fifth trophic level and receives 2 kJ.\nThe frog is at the third trophic level, two levels below the hawk.\nFormula: each lower level holds 10 times the energy of the level above it.\nSubstituting: the snake (fourth level) has 2 × 10 = 20 kJ, and the frog (third level) has 20 × 10 = 200 kJ.\nSo the energy at the frog's level is 200 kJ.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following:\nProductivity\nDecomposition\nStratification\nEnergy flow\nSpecies composition\nNutrient cycling\nHow many of these are the aspects through which the components of an ecosystem are seen to function as a unit?","image":"","options":["Three","Five","Four","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four functional aspects of an ecosystem: productivity, decomposition, energy flow and nutrient cycling.\nStratification and species composition are structural features, not functional aspects.\nSo 4 of the 6 items qualify.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Oceans occupy about 70 per cent of the Earth's surface and contribute more than half of the biosphere's annual net primary productivity of about 170 billion tons.\nStatement II: In an aquatic ecosystem, the grazing food chain is the major conduit for energy flow.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: Incorrect. Oceans cover about 70% of the surface, but their productivity is only about 55 of the 170 billion tons (dry weight). The rest comes from land.\nStatement II: Correct. The grazing food chain (GFC) is the major energy conduit in aquatic ecosystems. In terrestrial ecosystems, a larger fraction flows through the detritus food chain.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Producers belong to the first trophic level.\n(ii) In some ecosystems, the energy at a higher trophic level exceeds that at the lower trophic level.\n(iii) Standing crop can be expressed as the number of organisms in a unit area.\n(iv) Calculations of energy or biomass at a trophic level must include all organisms at that level.\n(v) Primary carnivores are secondary consumers.\n(vi) Human beings function only at the second trophic level.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: producers form the first trophic level.\n(ii) Incorrect: energy at a lower trophic level is always more than at a higher level.\n(iii) Correct: standing crop is measured as biomass or as number per unit area.\n(iv) Correct: generalisations based on only a few individuals are not valid.\n(v) Correct: animals eating herbivores are primary carnivores but secondary consumers.\n(vi) Incorrect: humans are omnivores and function at more than one trophic level.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An animal that feeds on herbivores is a primary carnivore but a secondary consumer.\nStatement II: Decomposers of the detritus food chain are autotrophic organisms that obtain energy from dead organic matter.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. An animal eating herbivores is the first carnivore in the chain, so it is a primary carnivore. Its food passes through two links (producer, then herbivore), so it is a secondary consumer.\nStatement II is incorrect. Decomposers (fungi, bacteria) are heterotrophic, not autotrophic. They degrade detritus to meet their energy and nutrient needs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Pyramid of numbers in a grassland\n(b) Pyramid of biomass in the sea\n(c) Pyramid of energy\n(d) Pyramid of numbers of insects feeding on a single big tree\nList-II\n(i) Small standing crop of phytoplankton supports a large standing crop of zooplankton\n(ii) Always upright because energy is lost as heat at each step\n(iii) Millions of plants support only a few top carnivores\n(iv) A single producer supports a very large number of herbivores","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): the grassland pyramid has about 6 million plants supporting 3 top carnivores.\n(b)-(i): the sea biomass pyramid is inverted, with a small phytoplankton standing crop and a large zooplankton standing crop.\n(c)-(ii): the energy pyramid is always upright because heat is lost at each transfer.\n(d)-(iv): one tree supports many insects, so the base of the number pyramid is narrow.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"In a forest, trees occupy the top layer, shrubs the second layer, and herbs and grasses the bottom layers. What is this vertical distribution of species called?","image":"","options":["Species composition","Stratification","Standing crop","Trophic level"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the vertical distribution of different species occupying different levels is called stratification.\nThe forest layers of trees, shrubs and herbs are the NCERT example of stratification.\nSpecies composition is the tempting wrong option. It means identifying and listing the species present, not arranging them vertically.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Catabolism of detritus is carried out mainly by the digestive action of detritivores such as earthworms.\nStatement II: In leaching, water-soluble inorganic nutrients are precipitated in the soil horizon as unavailable salts.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Catabolism is the degradation of detritus by bacterial and fungal enzymes into simpler inorganic substances. Earthworms are responsible for fragmentation, not catabolism.\nStatement II is correct. It matches NCERT's description of leaching.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Measuring the biomass of a trophic level in terms of dry weight is more accurate than measuring it as fresh weight.\nReason (R): The water content of organisms varies with conditions and time, and water does not represent organic matter or stored energy.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that measuring biomass as dry weight is more accurate.\nR is true. Water content fluctuates, for example with hydration, season or time of sampling.\nWater also carries no organic matter or energy, so fresh weight gives unreliable and misleading values.\nRemoving water leaves only the organic matter, which is a stable measure. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) GPP − R = NPP\n(ii) Productivity is expressed in g $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$ or kcal $m^{-2}$ $yr^{-1}$\n(iii) Net primary productivity is the biomass available for consumption by herbivores and decomposers\n(iv) Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by producers\n(v) Primary production is expressed in terms of weight (g $m^{-2}$) or energy (kcal $m^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: net primary productivity equals gross primary productivity minus respiration losses.\n(ii) Correct: these are the units used to compare the productivity of ecosystems.\n(iii) Correct: NPP is the biomass available to heterotrophs.\n(iv) Incorrect: secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers, not producers.\n(v) Correct: production, as an amount, is expressed in g $m^{-2}$ or kcal $m^{-2}$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-ECOSYSTEM-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ecosystem","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ecosystem","type":"mcq","question":"A grassland receives 1,000,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ of incident solar radiation in a season, of which 45% is PAR. The plants capture 4% of this PAR. If only 10 per cent of the energy fixed by producers reaches herbivores, how much energy is available to the herbivores?","image":"","options":["18,000 kcal m$^{-2}$","4,500 kcal m$^{-2}$","1,800 kcal m$^{-2}$","180 kcal m$^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: incident radiation = $10^6$ kcal m$^{-2}$; PAR = 45%; capture = 4% of PAR; transfer to herbivores = 10%.\nPAR = $10^6 \\times 0.45 = 450000$ kcal m$^{-2}$.\nEnergy fixed by producers = $450000 \\times 0.04 = 18000$ kcal m$^{-2}$.\nEnergy reaching herbivores = $18000 \\times 0.10$ = 1,800 kcal m$^{-2}$.\nThe value 18,000 kcal m$^{-2}$ is a trap: it is the producer level, before the 10% transfer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 12 · Ecosystem","difficulty":"hard"}]});
